family: rsyslog
what: syslog
transport: tls
address: logs-01.example.net
verification: tested
verification_detail: CI saves this file for real rsyslog, over UDP, TCP and TLS (Woodshed's own CA), over IPv4 and over IPv6 (TCP), with a chosen severity and facility (UDP), and with Woodshed down until the queue overflows (TCP and TLS). The test messages go to rsyslog's own UDP input rather than through the logger line.
reference: rsyslog documentation: the omfwd action (target, port, protocol, TCP_Framing, StreamDriver*), RFC 5424 and RFC 5425
does: Forwards this machine's syslog (local4 only), notice and more severe, to Woodshed over TLS.
check: On Woodshed's Dashboard this machine appears as a device within a few seconds of the test message; `woodshed status` counts the messages too.
placeholders: <CA-FILE>
addresses: 192.0.2.10 2001:db8::10 logs-01.example.net logs-01
notes:
- The test message uses facility user, which the local4 filter doesn't forward: use `logger -p local4.notice` to test.
- <CA-FILE> is Woodshed's CA certificate as a PEM file on this machine: download it from Settings → TLS → CA certificate (PEM), for example to /etc/rsyslog.d/woodshed-ca.pem (some distributions confine rsyslogd to reading its own configuration folders, so keep it there), and check its SHA-256 fingerprint is AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89:AB:CD:EF:01:23:45:67:89.
- rsyslog checks that Woodshed's certificate holds the name logs-01.example.net.
- TLS needs rsyslog 8.2108 or later and its gtls network stream driver, which some distributions package separately.
- While Woodshed can't be reached, up to 10,000 messages wait in the in-memory queue and rsyslog retries until it can. Once the queue is full, new messages for Woodshed are dropped (queue.timeoutEnqueue="0") rather than holding up the rest of the system's logging; local log files carry on, and a restart of rsyslog loses what's waiting.
---- part file "/etc/rsyslog.d/60-woodshed.conf": Save as this file, then restart rsyslog (sudo systemctl restart rsyslog)
# Woodshed: forward syslog over TLS (RFC 5424 format).
local4.notice action(type="omfwd" target="logs-01.example.net" port="6514" protocol="tcp" TCP_Framing="octet-counted"
       StreamDriver="gtls" StreamDriverMode="1" StreamDriver.CAFile="<CA-FILE>"
       StreamDriverAuthMode="x509/name" StreamDriverPermittedPeers="logs-01.example.net"
       template="RSYSLOG_SyslogProtocol23Format"
       queue.type="LinkedList" queue.size="10000" queue.timeoutEnqueue="0" action.resumeRetryCount="-1")
---- part command "": Check the file and send a test message
sudo rsyslogd -N1
logger -p user.notice "Test message for Woodshed from $(hostname)"
==== text
# /etc/rsyslog.d/60-woodshed.conf: Save as this file, then restart rsyslog (sudo systemctl restart rsyslog)
# Woodshed: forward syslog over TLS (RFC 5424 format).
local4.notice action(type="omfwd" target="logs-01.example.net" port="6514" protocol="tcp" TCP_Framing="octet-counted"
       StreamDriver="gtls" StreamDriverMode="1" StreamDriver.CAFile="<CA-FILE>"
       StreamDriverAuthMode="x509/name" StreamDriverPermittedPeers="logs-01.example.net"
       template="RSYSLOG_SyslogProtocol23Format"
       queue.type="LinkedList" queue.size="10000" queue.timeoutEnqueue="0" action.resumeRetryCount="-1")

# Check the file and send a test message
sudo rsyslogd -N1
logger -p user.notice "Test message for Woodshed from $(hostname)"
